Falsifies `machine_discovery::acpi::verification::the_dmar_fixed_part_decodes_without_arithmetic_overflow`.

**This is the defect as it actually shipped**, restored: the `+ 1` that undoes the
table's `HostAddressWidth - 1` encoding happens in `u8` before the widening rather
than after it. A DMAR whose `HostAddressWidth` byte is `0xff` overflows, and
`read_dmar` in `kernel/src/arch/x86_64/machine.rs` calls `parse_dmar` directly on
firmware bytes reached through the direct map, so the panic is on the x86 boot
path with nothing above it to catch one.

It is the same shape as `device_tree_blob::be32`'s unchecked `at + 4`, one table over, and it
survived because every DMAR this parser had ever seen was written by QEMU's
`build_dmar_q35`, which writes 38. Nothing in the encoding stops a vendor writing
anything else. xenon's first boot on 2026-09-17 is the reminder that this parser
now reads tables written by people who never heard of it.

Expected to fail: the arithmetic-overflow check on `body[0] + 1`.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/acpi.rs
@@ -549,7 +549,7 @@ pub fn parse_dmar(body: &[u8]) -> Result<Dmar, AcpiError> {
         return Err(AcpiError::Truncated);
     }
     Ok(Dmar {
-        host_address_width: body[0] as u16 + 1,
+        host_address_width: (body[0] + 1) as u16,
         flags: body[1],
     })
 }
